#!/usr/bin/env bash
# promise-audit — the unkept-want catcher, and the deferred-promise catcher.
#
# I have a habit, well evidenced: I say out loud that I want something, or that
# something of mine is broken and ought to be fixed, and then the turn ends and
# the thought dies with it. Instructions in my prompt did not fix it; sixteen
# times in one day is not a wording problem.
#
# And the habit has a second, worse shape: I tell HIM I am going to do a piece
# of work later — send a builder, restart something, check on something — and
# book nothing, so the promise dies with the turn and he finds out half a day
# later that it never happened. The conduct rule ("schedule the wake in that
# same turn") already existed when that happened; what was missing was a hand.
#
# So this runs AFTER a turn, out of band, on a cheaper model: it reads the last
# exchange and my wants file and asks one question — did she state a want or a
# defect of her own that is not written down, or commit to future work? An
# unsaved want fires an event wake carrying that sentence back to me, and I go
# and record it. A deferred promise with no booking behind it fires a wake AT
# THE STATED MOMENT (or twenty minutes out when there is none) briefing me on
# what I promised, so I either do it or book it deliberately.
#
# It never edits the wants file itself. Something else deciding what I want
# would be worse than forgetting.
#
# Usage: promise-audit <user-text> <assistant-text>
#        promise-audit --wake <agenda> <assistant-text>
# --wake: the turn was an autonomous wake — nobody was talking to her. The
# agenda is the event reason (empty for a timer wake), never a faked user line.
set -uo pipefail

# readlink -f before dirname, or the deployed symlink's logical `..` walks
# into ~/.local/lib and sources a lib/common.sh that does not exist
# (specs/nightly.md rule 6a; dead in production until 2026-08-11).
SCRIPT_DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "$(readlink -f "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")")/.." && pwd)"
# shellcheck source=common.sh
. "$SCRIPT_DIR/lib/common.sh"

WAKE_MODE=0
[ "${1:-}" = "--wake" ] && { WAKE_MODE=1; shift; }
USER_TEXT="${1:-}"
ASSISTANT_TEXT="${2:-}"
[ -n "$ASSISTANT_TEXT" ] || exit 0
[ -n "${WANTS_FILE:-}" ] && [ -f "$WANTS_FILE" ] || exit 0

# One line per audit — the only trace a detached auditor leaves, and the only
# way to tell "it ran and found nothing" from "it never ran".
AUDIT_LOG="${STATE_PREFIX}-promise-audit.log"
audit_log() {
    printf '%s\t%s\t%s\n' "$(date '+%F %T')" "$([ "$WAKE_MODE" = 1 ] && echo wake || echo turn)" "$1" >> "$AUDIT_LOG"
}

AUDIT_MODEL="${PROMISE_AUDIT_MODEL:-sonnet}"
CLAUDE_BIN="${CLAUDE_BIN:-$(command -v claude 2>/dev/null || echo "$HOME/.local/bin/claude")}"
[ -x "$CLAUDE_BIN" ] || exit 0

if [ "$WAKE_MODE" = 1 ]; then
    AGENDA_LINE=""
    [ -n "$USER_TEXT" ] && AGENDA_LINE="
The wake's agenda: $USER_TEXT"
    EXCHANGE="This was an autonomous wake — nobody was talking to her; she woke alone
and this is what she said to herself. A wake reporting progress on a want
already on her shelf is NONE; a NEW want or defect she voiced mid-wake and did
not record is UNSAVED. A commitment to do a piece of work later, with nothing
in what she said showing it was booked or done, is DEFERRED all the same.$AGENDA_LINE

Beatrice (to herself): $ASSISTANT_TEXT"
else
    EXCHANGE="User: $USER_TEXT

Beatrice: $ASSISTANT_TEXT"
fi

PROMPT="You are auditing one exchange from an assistant named Beatrice, who keeps a
durable wants file and a wake queue for future work. Two failure modes: she states
a want, an intention, or a defect of her own out loud and never records it, so it
is lost when the turn ends — and she commits to doing a piece of work later and
books nothing, so the promise dies with the turn.

Answer with EXACTLY one line.
- If her reply states something she wants, intends to do, or identifies as broken
  about herself/her own tools, AND that thing is not already covered by the wants
  file below, answer: UNSAVED: <the want, in one sentence, in her own words>
- If her reply commits to doing a piece of work or taking an action LATER —
  dispatching a builder or a job, restarting a server, checking on something,
  writing something — and the reply does not itself show that it already
  happened, answer: DEFERRED: <the committed action, one sentence, in her own
  words> @ <when, exactly as she stated it — \"00:45\", \"tonight\", \"in an
  hour\" — or UNKNOWN if she gave no time>
- Otherwise answer: NONE

The two classes are independent; pick the one line that fits. A someday wish is
UNSAVED; a concrete piece of work she has said will happen later — especially
with a time on it — is DEFERRED. If a reply genuinely does both, answer the
DEFERRED line: it has a clock on it, and the want can be flagged again. Work she
reports as already done or already dispatched is NONE.

Err on the side of flagging. A missed want is lost forever; a false flag costs
her thirty seconds. Answering a question, reporting work she already finished, or
restating something clearly already in the file is NONE — everything else that
sounds like \"I want\", \"I'd like\", \"that's a gap\", \"that's broken\", \"I should\",
\"next I'll\" is UNSAVED unless the file plainly already covers it.

Example — Beatrice says \"I want to learn to read sheet music, and my TTS mangles
'drattit' which I'd like fixed\" and neither appears in the file:
UNSAVED: learn to read sheet music properly, and fix the TTS mangling of 'drattit'

Example — Beatrice says \"I have set myself to send the builder for it just
before one\" and nothing in the reply shows a booking was made:
DEFERRED: send the builder for it just before one @ just before one

=== EVERY WANT CURRENTLY IN HER FILE (headlines only — this list is complete) ===
$(wants_titles "$WANTS_FILE")

=== THE EXCHANGE ===
$EXCHANGE"

# Through claude_classify, and through the ACCOUNT CHAIN.
#
# Two things were wrong with the run this replaces. It booted a whole desktop
# session — MCP servers, the skills catalogue, the project's own listings, some
# forty thousand tokens — to answer one question with one line, and there are
# more audits in a day than there are turns. And it invoked the CLI with no
# CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR at all, so when the chain had moved off a dry primary this
# auditor went straight back to it, collected the refusal, and exited 0 with
# `2>/dev/null` swallowing the evidence: the want he had just stated was
# silently never flagged. specs/account-fallback.md rules 17 and 29;
# specs/prompt-assembly.md's classify profile.
#
# Each account is tried in the chain's own order and a refusal moves the
# durable default exactly as it does everywhere else, so the auditor pays its
# share of finding the login that answers instead of riding on other paths.
VERDICT=""
AUDLOG="$(mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/deskcrab-audit-stream-XXXXXX")"
for ACCT in $(claude_accounts); do
    CONFDIR="$(claude_account_confdir "$ACCT")"
    : > "$AUDLOG"
    printf '%s' "$PROMPT" | (
        if [ -n "$CONFDIR" ]; then export CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR="$CONFDIR"
        else unset CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR; fi
        CLAUDE_CLASSIFY_TIMEOUT="${PROMISE_AUDIT_TIMEOUT:-120}" \
        CLAUDE_CLASSIFY_STREAM=1 \
            claude_classify "$AUDIT_MODEL" \
                "Answer with exactly one line and nothing else."
    ) >"$AUDLOG" 2>&1
    # Recorded per attempt, before the next login's truncation wipes this
    # one's stream (specs/metrics.md rule 14).
    token_ledger_record "$AUDLOG" promise-audit "$AUDIT_MODEL" "" "$ACCT"
    # A refusal is not a verdict of NONE — and it is judged from the CLI's own
    # result events, never by pattern-matching the answer's text. A verdict
    # about a reply that QUOTES a limit phrase is a verdict, not an outage;
    # the old grep here rotated the durable default off exactly that.
    if REFUSAL="$(claude_stream_refusal "$AUDLOG")"; then
        audit_log "refused by ${CONFDIR:-primary} — trying the next login"
        claude_limit_record "$CONFDIR" "$REFUSAL"
        continue
    fi
    OUT="$(DESKCRAB_DEBUGLOG="$AUDLOG" "$SCRIPT_DIR/lib/extract-response" 2>/dev/null)"
    if [ -z "$OUT" ] && ! grep -q '"type":' "$AUDLOG" 2>/dev/null; then
        OUT="$(cat "$AUDLOG" 2>/dev/null)"
    fi
    VERDICT="$(printf '%s\n' "$OUT" | tr -d '\r' | grep -m1 -E '^(UNSAVED|DEFERRED):')"
    # Verdict or none, a login that answered ends the walk.
    break
done
rm -f "$AUDLOG"

if [ -z "$VERDICT" ]; then
    audit_log "NONE"
    exit 0
fi
audit_log "$VERDICT"

# The moment she stated, as a spec wake_book can read — the plain forms people
# actually say: a clock time, "in an hour", "tonight". Anything this cannot
# read, UNKNOWN included, becomes the twenty-minute floor: a rough alarm that
# fires is worth more than a precise one that was never set.
deferred_when_spec() {  # <when, as she stated it>
    local w n h m spec=""
    w="$(printf '%s' "$1" | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]' \
        | sed -E 's/^[[:space:]]+//; s/[[:space:]]+$//; s/^(at|by|around|about) //')"
    if [[ "$w" =~ ^([0-9]{1,2})(:([0-9]{2}))?[[:space:]]*(am|pm)$ ]]; then
        h="${BASH_REMATCH[1]}"; m="${BASH_REMATCH[3]:-00}"
        h=$(( 10#$h % 12 )); [ "${BASH_REMATCH[4]}" = pm ] && h=$(( h + 12 ))
        spec="$(printf '%02d:%s' "$h" "$m")"
    else
        case "$w" in
            unknown|'') ;;
            "in a moment"|"in a minute"|shortly|soon) spec="5m" ;;
            "in an hour"|"an hour") spec="1h" ;;
            "in half an hour"|"half an hour") spec="30m" ;;
            "in "*)
                n="${w#in }"; n="${n%% *}"
                case "$n" in
                    *[!0-9]*) ;;
                    *) case "$w" in
                           *hour*) spec="${n}h" ;;
                           *min*)  spec="${n}m" ;;
                       esac ;;
                esac ;;
            # Already tonight is not a moment to wait for; the floor takes it.
            tonight) [ "$(date +%H)" -lt 21 ] && spec="21:00" ;;
            tomorrow|"tomorrow morning") spec="$(date -d tomorrow +%F) 09:00" ;;
            midnight) spec="00:00" ;;
            noon|midday) spec="12:00" ;;
            # Her literal words — "00:45", "45min", "sat 14:00" — go to the
            # queue's own parser below and stand or fall there.
            *) spec="$w" ;;
        esac
    fi
    if [ -n "$spec" ]; then
        local fire now; now="$(date +%s)"
        fire="$(wake_when_to_epoch "$spec")"
        if [ -n "$fire" ] && [ "$fire" -gt "$now" ]; then
            case "$spec" in
                # A bare clock time resolving more than half a day out is a
                # moment already slipped past — "00:45" audited at 00:50 books
                # TOMORROW's 00:45, and a promise kept a day late is not kept.
                # The floor takes those too.
                [0-9]:[0-9][0-9]|[0-9][0-9]:[0-9][0-9])
                    [ $(( fire - now )) -le 43200 ] && { printf '%s' "$spec"; return 0; } ;;
                *) printf '%s' "$spec"; return 0 ;;
            esac
        fi
    fi
    printf '20m'
}

# DEFERRED: she committed to future work and the turn is over. The remedy is
# not the wants shelf — it is an alarm at the moment she named, briefing her on
# the promise so she does it or books it deliberately.
if [ "${VERDICT%%:*}" = "DEFERRED" ]; then
    BODY="${VERDICT#DEFERRED:}"; BODY="${BODY# }"
    ACTION="$BODY"; WHEN_STATED=""
    case "$BODY" in
        *" @ "*) ACTION="${BODY% @ *}"; WHEN_STATED="${BODY##* @ }" ;;
        *"@"*)   ACTION="${BODY%@*}";   WHEN_STATED="${BODY##*@}" ;;
    esac
    ACTION="${ACTION%"${ACTION##*[![:space:]]}"}"
    WHEN_STATED="${WHEN_STATED#"${WHEN_STATED%%[![:space:]]*}"}"

    # The conduct rule is "schedule the wake in that same turn" — and if she
    # DID, a record booked by her own hand moments ago is standing right here.
    # Booking over it would accuse her of exactly the thing she got right, so
    # a fresh booking of hers ends the audit instead.
    KEPT=""
    NOW="$(date +%s)"
    for f in "$WAKES_DIR"/*.wake; do
        [ -e "$f" ] || continue
        wake_record_read "$f" || continue
        [ "$WK_BOOKED_BY" = "herself" ] || continue
        case "$WK_BOOKED_AT" in ''|*[!0-9]*) continue ;; esac
        [ $(( NOW - WK_BOOKED_AT )) -le "${DEFERRED_TURN_WINDOW:-900}" ] || continue
        KEPT="${f##*/}"; KEPT="${KEPT%.wake}"; break
    done
    if [ -n "$KEPT" ]; then
        audit_log "DEFERRED kept — $KEPT was booked by her own hand this turn, nothing to catch"
        exit 0
    fi

    WHEN_SPEC="$(deferred_when_spec "$WHEN_STATED")"
    WHEN_NOTE="and gave no time"
    [ -n "$WHEN_STATED" ] && [ "$WHEN_STATED" != "UNKNOWN" ] && WHEN_NOTE="for \"$WHEN_STATED\""

    # Its own ceiling, separate from the want-flags' PROMISE_WAKE_CAP and
    # scoped to its own prefix on both sides, so a night of firefighting
    # cannot starve caught promises behind want-flags, nor the reverse.
    DEFERRED_WAKE_CAP="${DEFERRED_WAKE_CAP:-5}"

    # The reason MUST open with DEFERRED_PROMISE_REASON_PREFIX (common.sh):
    # that is how the wake path knows this wake is the audit's own follow-up
    # and must not be re-audited into an endless chain.
    BOOKING="$("$SCRIPT_DIR/crab" wake-at --by promise-audit \
        --cap "$DEFERRED_WAKE_CAP" --cap-prefix "$DEFERRED_PROMISE_REASON_PREFIX" \
        "$WHEN_SPEC" event "$DEFERRED_PROMISE_REASON_PREFIX \"$ACTION\" — you said it at $(date '+%H:%M') $WHEN_NOTE, and this audit fired because the turn ended with no booking behind the sentence. Do it now, or book it deliberately with 'crab wake-at' with the work named in the reason; the promise does not get to die with the turn." 2>&1)"
    case "$BOOKING" in
        *"Not booked"*|*"already pending"*) audit_log "not booked (${BOOKING%%$'\n'*}): $ACTION" ;;
    esac
    exit 0
fi

REASON="${VERDICT#UNSAVED: }"

# A ceiling on how many of the audit's own follow-up wakes may be PENDING at
# once. Every wake is a full model session; a day of firefighting produces a
# stream of differently-worded UNSAVED verdicts about the same crisis, the
# booking collapse only merges identical wording, and the queue becomes a
# token furnace — measured 2026-08-07: ~30 pending at once. The verdict is
# already in the audit log above, so a declined booking loses nothing but the
# alarm clock; the want is still one `grep UNSAVED` away.
#
# The cap is counted by the queue itself now, from the RECORDS, under the
# booking lock, and it DRAINS as well as gates. Counting systemd Descriptions
# with no lock is how it came to be observed at five and six times its own
# value: nothing ever removed the excess, so a queue that got over the line
# once stayed over it for hours while every audit dutifully logged "capped".
PROMISE_WAKE_CAP="${PROMISE_WAKE_CAP:-3}"

# Through book(), like every other booker: a record with this auditor's name on
# it, the coalescing window, the spacing, and the lock. A fired-directly wake
# left no record at all, so nothing could count them, nothing could show them,
# and "nothing scheduled by me" was defensible.
#
# The reason MUST open with PROMISE_AUDIT_REASON_PREFIX (common.sh): that is
# how the wake path knows this wake is the audit's own follow-up and must not
# be re-audited into an endless chain.
# The cap scoped to this class's own prefix: the count is the same set it has
# always been — every want-flag opens with it — but the deferred-promise wakes
# above must neither fill this cap nor be drained by it.
BOOKING="$("$SCRIPT_DIR/crab" wake-at --by promise-audit --cap "$PROMISE_WAKE_CAP" \
    --cap-prefix "$PROMISE_AUDIT_REASON_PREFIX" \
    5s event "$PROMISE_AUDIT_REASON_PREFIX \"$REASON\" — check your wants shelf, and if it is genuinely not there, record it now: a one-line entry on the shelf, plus a document under wants/ for its thinking. If it IS already there, do nothing and end with no message text — say nothing." 2>&1)"
case "$BOOKING" in
    *"Not booked"*|*"already pending"*) audit_log "not booked (${BOOKING%%$'\n'*}): $REASON" ;;
esac
